\section{Why it is worth checking separately}{

\code{\link[=check_statement]{check_statement()}} follows the presentation hierarchy, which runs down the
statement. A breakdown runs the other way, across a dimension, and nothing
in the hierarchy reaches it. It is also where a particular error hides.

A filing in a sample of 300 Greenlandic companies tags its balance sheet in
kroner and its fixed-asset note in thousands, in the same document. Its
additions for the year read \code{229} and \code{174} when they are 229,000 and
174,000 kr, which would have entered an investment series at a thousandth of
their size. Nothing else catches it: the unit is \code{iso4217:DKK} like every
other filing's, \code{decimals} is consistent, and the company's registered
capital clears its legal floor because the \emph{balance sheet} is right. Only
the note is wrong, and only the filing's own arithmetic shows it.

So the ratio is the point, not the difference. It is \code{1} when the breakdown
agrees, \code{1000} or \code{0.001} when the two sit on different scales, and
something near but not equal to 1 when the breakdown does not cover every
member.
}

